e situation and it's only  getting worse now  and the comments from the un really  can the international community do  anything  they can do a lot and they have they  should have been doing a lot up  until now you know we know the  government has that so many times they  keep calling it an impulse war  they keep saying that there are  sanctuaries and that there are havens  and support in pakistan  they say that their support in iran you  know these are countries that can put  uh influence and pressure on these  neighboring countries that are  influencing directly  in the war in afghanistan and that are  supplying and arming and  training you know these militant forces  so  there's a lot that they could have been  doing and there's a lot that they can  still be doing and then  in terms of the negotiations there's no  reason that these countries cannot put  pressure on the taliban  to say and the government to say you  have to take these negotiations  seriously  you know we put you up in doha you've  been there since 2011. the government  has been there for the last year  one of you has to start making some real  moves now so  you know it's a lot for them to keep  making statements and condemning things  but they actually have to take action  things that they haven't done over the  last 20 years  i think a lot of people are wondering  how seriously we should consider the  possibility of a complete collapse of  the afghan government and the  establishment of a taliban islamist  state  i personally don't see it happening  because i feel like if you look at what  the taliban has been doing over the last  year and a half two years  they've been going on these diplomatic  missions around the world they've been  going to the neighboring countries  they've been meeting with european  uh american officials all the time and  you know they put out statements saying  you can take your troops but keep your  money here i really don't think that  they want to be a pariah state again  like they were in 1996.  i think they still want some level of  support and legitimacy  so for them to come and overrun call  ball and to start killing people on mats  and like  you know bringing back these draconian  rules  i don't think that's gonna fly again and  hopefully they're realistic enough to  know  that you know the only way through is  some kind of a settlement i mean right  now they're probably trying to put  pressure on everybody  through the war but hopefully they  accept the reality that the only way  forward is the settlement  ali latifi thank you so much for joining  us  thank you afghanistan has  a long history of local militias  switching sides and allegiances  depending on the tide of politics  the sangria militiamen and helmand  province are currently fighting the  taliban  the force counts former insurgents among  their ranks  some say they've been trained by  afghanistan's spy agency as well as  foreign forces what's certain is that  for now  their resistance is stopping though  perhaps only delaying the capture of a  strategic city  gunfire at the gates of leicester the  capital of helmand province  as these defenders of the city fire on  taliban positions  this is the front line and the fighting  is going on every day  the situation isn't good  right now we're here firing all around  us  just days ago i was injured in the  battle  we're fighting the taliban here at  extremely close quarters  these are the sangorians a fierce  fighting force and  hated by the insurgents the militia were  established in 2015  taking its name from a turkey soap opera  about  undercover agents  many here were recruited from the  taliban their mission  to infiltrate and disrupt the insurgency  but for some sangorians the fight is  personal  the taliban are retaking more and more  of afghanistan ahead of the u.s  withdrawal  the sangorians face an enemy that's  better equipped  and growing bolder by the day  there is no need  the taliban use modern weapons like  sniper rifles with night vision  and they come in very close to target  i was injured but i survived  is holding the line  entrances to the city  local officials acknowledge the vital  role of the sangorians in keeping the  taliban at bay  city  sangorians are having a big impact on  the security of helmand province  we're asking the government the ministry  of defence and the national security  director  to recruit more fighters for the  sangorians  to defend helmand province  but that help may never arrive with  government forces on the back foot  and soon to lose american support the  sangorians know they're fighting with  nothing to lose  italy is warning that troop withdrawal  could prompt many afghans to flee  to europe the german port town of  hamburg is already home to a vibrant uh  community of afghan  migrants they're worried about what the  pull-out will mean for  their home country and for relatives  back home  mariam nature and yama wazir live here  in the bilstead neighborhood of hamburg  this district houses one of europe's  largest communities of exiled afghans  nearly forty thousand of whom live here  you can get lamb  fruit and vegetables and afghan baked  goods  and her family fled from afghanistan  when she was just a child  they're really yummy too these cookies  are soft and delicious  she enjoys the traditions of her  homeland but also tells us that exiled  afghans fear the return of the radical  islamist taliban  we get news especially when we call our  families we ask  are you all healthy is everyone still  alive  that's the biggest worry we have so far  fortunately  none of us has been affected but of  course that's a matter of luck  something can always happen  the afghan women's association knows  these worries only too well  for almost 30 years the mostly volunteer  group has been working on projects in  afghanistan  they concentrate on schools clinics and  water supply  chairwoman nadia nashiya is critical of  the rapid withdrawal o