ink the only thing where we  have  maybe met our expectations was in  fighting international terrorism so  afghanistan no longer poses a threat or  um jihadi groups no longer pose an  international threat to europe or to the  uh united uh states so  the the country's insecure and corrupt  and has no infrastructure  presumably the troops are leaving this  is purely because um  donald trump decided to withdraw u.s  troops  so everybody else followed suit  well i would not blame it only on on  president trump's decision  but of course we were quite surprised  how fast the troops  then were leaving and how quick and  unconditioned the u.s troops  um decided to withdraw so this came  quite as a surprise but i think  generally i would say that  all international forces in the country  founded time to leave  maybe a bit later but it was not a  question of  whether to leave but rather how and when  to leave that was the main question  so is there a plan either from  international partners or within  afghanistan is there a plan for how to  defeat corruption how to build security  and how to build an infrastructure  once the international presence has gone  well this is very difficult to say  because we have been  claiming or praising afghan ownership  and afghan-led and afghan-owned  construction and peace process since 20  years  and but on the other hand we were kind  of telling them how to do it  and i think now we have come to the  point where where we really say or very  really see what it means afghan  ownership  and i think now it's time to  also have the confidence in the afghans  to build up their country but of course  they need our solidarity  but now of course without their military  strength and the military international  forces in the country  okay thank you for joining us dr dr  elena zino from the conrad adenauer  foundation  thank you  you

